      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "In STEMI, grade-3 ischemia (G3) on admission ECG predicts larger infarct size (IS) than grade-2 (G2). We evaluated whether pre-hospital G3 and its temporal behavior are associated with IS and salvage after pPCI."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "In 401 STEMI patients, pre-hospital and pre-PCI ECGs were classified as G3 or G2. IS was assessed by single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) at 30days. In 245 patients, pre-PCI SPECT was available to determine myocardium at risk (MaR)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "G3 criteria were met by 88, and G2 by 313 patients. G3 was independently associated with IS (p=0.006). With ST resolution (STR) group as a reference, G2->G2, G2->G3 and G3->G3 were associated with larger IS (B=4.4, p=0.004; B=5.4, p=0.01; B=10.2, p<0.001, respectively), whereas G3->G2 was not. Salvage was similar between G3 and G2 on pre-hospital ECG if treated early, however lower for G3 when treated later (>2.5h); 48% (35-78) vs 62% (40-87); p=0.04."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Development or persistence of G3 is associated with larger IS and less salvage, but decreasing grade G3->G2 was not."
          }
        ]
      },
